WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.417
JENNY BROWN: So
far, we've learned

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:02.850
that Critter
Junction has decided

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:05.460
to go with Cloud Run
for their layout app.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.370
Will they learn to optimize
Cloud Run for scalability?

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:08.925
Stay tuned.

00:00:08.925 --> 00:00:11.076
[THEME MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:20.220
To refresh, the layout app
is a key part of the game.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.970
You can share house
layouts with other players.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:25.980
And since containerizing
their Node.js application,

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.660
they've decided
to run it on Cloud

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.360
Run for its portability,
statelessness,

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.880
and auto-scaling, even to zero.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.790
And unlike their online
site running on App Engine,

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:38.580
they haven't needed to write
warm up wrappers in their code,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.640
because Cloud Run may keep
some idle instances around

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.800
to handle spikes in traffic.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.340
The thing is, Cloud Run will
terminate unused Cloud Run

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.700
containers after some
time, which means

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.070
a cold start can still occur.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.650
After looking at recent
deployments of the layout app,

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.750
we noticed a few things
that could be improved

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.030
to minimize cold start latency.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:03.090
First, they happened to be
using a dynamic language

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:07.380
with dependent libraries, like
importing modules and Node.js.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.360
They weren't using
global variables,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.190
and their container-based
images were

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.370
about 700 megabytes in size.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.820
This meant that overall, they
were facing longer load times

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.420
upon container startup or
required additional computation

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:24.720
before the server could
start listening for requests.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.930
Instead, they want to
optimize their service startup

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:31.320
speed to minimize the
latency that causes these.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.450
Let's dive into each.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:37.200
For starters, on Cloud Run, the
size of your container image

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.520
does not affect cold start
or request processing time.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.310
But you want to be careful
when it comes to applications

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.800
written in dynamic languages.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.460
For example, if you're
using Node.js or Python,

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.070
module loading that happens
on processed startup

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.560
will add latency
during a cold start.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.100
Also, be aware of some modules
that run initialization code

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.440
upon importing.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.690
To build a leaner
service, you can

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.300
minimize the number and
size of dependencies

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.690
if you're using a dynamic
language, instead of computing

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:13.200
things upon startup,
compute them lazily,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.840
shorten your initializations
and speed up time

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.790
to start your HTTP server, and
use code loading optimizations

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:25.080
like PHP's Composer
auto-loader optimization.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.630
Next up, global variables.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:30.190
In Cloud Run, you can't
assume that service state

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.250
is preserved between requests.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.790
But Cloud Run does reuse
individual container instances

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:38.200
to serve ongoing traffic.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.810
That means you can
declare a global variable,

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:44.980
so when new containers are spun
up, it can reuse its value.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.850
You can also cache
objects in memory.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:51.270
Moving this from the storage
request logic to global scope

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.280
means better performance.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.730
A lot of this boils down to
creating a leaner service.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.410
You want to build
a minimal container

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:02.280
by working off of a lean base
image like Alpine, Distroless,

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:03.870
or Scratch.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.540
These images reduced Critter
Junction's image size

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.230
from 700 megabytes
to 65 megabytes.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:13.170
They also made sure to only
install what was strictly

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.180
needed inside the image.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.700
In other words, don't
install extra packages

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.380
that you don't need.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.800
Once Critter Junction was able
to remove dependencies, use

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.920
global variables, swap
to a leaner base image,

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.170
and remove extra
packages, they were

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:32.140
able to reduce the latency
of any Cloud Run cold starts.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.350
Now, there are
other best practices

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.180
for Cloud Run scalability
and performance,

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:39.450
so be sure to check
out the link below.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:40.410
Stay tuned.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.420
And remember, always
be architecting.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.770
